# Project knowledge and verification supplements

Stage reads run through `flow context`. In `constitution`, `stage_recall` reads only the local native project principles and published Birdview rules catalog. Retain the actual stage, `understand`, binding, and original catalog response. Page through the fixed catalog scope regardless of whether a task title matches. This stage skips relevance-ranked business knowledge recall, full knowledge documents, and their business Verifiers. Any separately configured active required project context still follows [the three project supplements](optional-project-context.md) and is recorded separately from business knowledge recall. Report rule counts and knowledge hits separately. Business knowledge recall is inapplicable in `constitution`.

In `specify`, `clarify`, and `plan`, read relevant knowledge for the current requirement or technical question. Continue reading the published project rules catalog separately. Base the query on the original request and earlier requirements and plans. A stage query with source evidence can also be supplied under `stages` in `recall-query.json`. Read every page of fallback queries too. Expand knowledge documents to the last page of the pinned version and preserve the actual associated Verifier's identity, status, and source. Definitions saved with the response can be examined later. Guidance without a verification method does not create acceptance items. Record additional reads at preview under preview and preserve earlier stages' actual read history.

When recall is configured, save a read record for each early stage before submitting that stage. Record unpublished content, a successful complete empty result, pagination failure, and permission failure separately. Continue local development when optional material fails to load and state the actual gap. Earlier recalled Verifiers remain in stage history if a later query returns no hits. When a source changes, reassess affected artifacts while retaining the original stage confirmation and failures. Adopting new definitions requires an explicit decision.

The online interfaces in this reference provide optional project material. See [the three project supplements](optional-project-context.md) for entry points, missing inputs, adoption, acceptance, and synchronization. Verifiers that were never recalled may be skipped. Applicable adopted checks that fail or remain unverified block acceptance. Pin the definitions actually read and use trusted local normalization so acceptance can finish without upload. Synchronization is an independent archival action.

Reuse the project's actual `knowledge_bridge.argv` from `birdview-bridge.json` and keep credentials in the CLI. When `recall.enabled:true`, verify each actual candidate repository's own online binding and read its `knowledge_recall_guidance`. Record failures, zero hits, and partial supply per repository. Reuse the snapshot for the same stage, query, and repository scope. For explicit retry, use `flow recall PROJECT --query 'task intent' --term 'keyword' --path 'relevant directory'`. Repeat `--term` and `--path` as needed. A feature's `recall-query.json` can retain the query and its derivation as `query`, `terms`, `paths`, and `source_refs`. The program records its digest. Paths are relevance preferences by default. With no explicit keyword, a complete Chinese title can serve as a bounded literal tokenization fallback. The fallback uses literal matching and does not perform semantic vector recall.

Preserve original responses, matched fields and paths, sources, conditions, exceptions, and budget gaps. When an older document lacks a trusted unit format, fall back to the complete document or original source entry. An ordinary heading does not establish an independent knowledge identity. If the response omits part of the text, save `knowledge_ref` to JSON and use `flow knowledge-source PROJECT --reference-file FILE --worktree REPOSITORY` to continue the read using the `source_read` `content_hash/run_id` when present. Assemble all native JSON pages before interpreting the text. Recall does not adopt knowledge. Continue reading the entire active required set under the same project's authorization and track it as the third project supplement. It creates no global gate or independent business-task prerequisite.

Verifiers accompany knowledge responses. During recall, show each Verifier's identity, generation status, and source association. When adopting a knowledge item, carry forward its original associated Verifier. Keep adoption separate from recall and preserve the association instead of selecting from all candidate Verifiers independently. A package association establishes neither applicability nor a pass for every check. At `finish/reverify`, read detailed definitions and decide individual applicability before execution. Read only definitions associated with actual adoptions through `flow verifier-read PROJECT --verifier-id ID --worktree REPOSITORY`. Reuse an already complete definition for the same version. Record generation failure, absence of checks, and read gaps explicitly.

Starting with knowledge recall in `specify/clarify/plan`, retain each associated Verifier and its complete provenance. Requirements, planning, and task analysis record actual adoptions and their application, then carry forward each adopted item's Verifier. Preview summarizes and displays those established associations. The stage does not require close reading of all candidates, an independent Verifier recommendation, or selection confirmation. The `constitution` rules catalog read does not expand business knowledge or create business Verifier candidates. Treat knowledge without a Verifier as guidance. Keep its prose out of the acceptance checklist unless acceptance has its own source. At `finish/reverify`, the current Codex session reads the adopted item's original checks against the actual code, repository version, and operating conditions. Save applicability and actual local bindings using the original `expected`, `applies_when`, `criteria`, and `methods`. Keep the original assertions. Handle later scope changes through the existing preview revision flow.

Use `flow verifier-candidates`, `verifier-recommend`, and `verifier-confirm` only when the user independently asks to adjust the verification set. Normal stages can proceed without these interactions. For a real `verifier_select` request, preserve the original selection, version, and source. Report preselections and drafts in their actual states. A failed adopted item requires a sourced finding of inapplicability, a scope-change reason, and an exclusion record before removing it from the set. Preserve earlier recommendations, selections, executions, and request digests.

Open already-read sources and definitions inside the card. The Workbench reads later saved source pages through its read-only interface without a model message. If a fixed conversation card omits those pages, direct the user to the local sidebar. Use the explicit “Request read” action to return an unread source to the current session.

- `constitution` summarizes built-in methods, local project principles, available project SOP and Birdview rules, and the current user input. Keep business knowledge recall out of the principles stage.
- `specify/clarify` use relevant knowledge as supporting evidence. Adopt only selected material and record unused material and conflicts. For incomplete text, continue the exact source through `knowledge-source` and identify excerpts as excerpts.
- `plan/tasks_analyze` carry actual adoption decisions, where each item applies, and the associated original Verifier into the unified list alongside requirement acceptance. Skip reading every Verifier in depth and separate recommendation or selection. Guidance without a Verifier adds no acceptance item. Keep ordinary project checks in preview `execution.checks`. Authorization to execute commands must come from the current scope. Server documents supply guidance.
- `preview` summarizes existing requirement acceptance and Verifiers for adopted knowledge. Show each source, original stage, pinned version, related task, and generation or read gap. Reuse explicit decisions already made. Avoid reopening every candidate, reassessing all items, or confirming the verification set again for preview. Save exact existing references through [the unified acceptance scope](acceptance-scope.md). The program can carry retained original definitions without having the model read each one for display. Current acceptance requires current evidence.
- `implement` executes existing tasks, preserves the knowledge-to-Verifier association, and continues existing project checks. Keep earlier candidate reading and selection decisions.
- `finish/reverify` read original Verifiers for adopted knowledge and determine applicability in the current code and environment. Save actual local bindings through `flow verifier-bind PROJECT --input FILE` and run `flow verifier-run PROJECT --operation-id KEY --expected-revision N`. Reuse pinned read definitions and original responses. Synchronization requires a separate decision. Use normal preview revision when command or implementation scope must expand. Report `program` and `model` separately against the current code, definition, and evidence. Missing methods, environment, or evidence remains `unknown`. Preserve program failures and the original failure records, then repair and reverify without changing original assertions. Completion requires a passing local `flow check` and current Verifier gate. A Server receipt is independent of local acceptance.

Supply model judgments during execution using current evidence. Do not predeclare them as planning passes. Optional `--evaluation FILE` uses the shape `{target_sha256,packages:{VERIFIER_ID:{model_input,model_results,evidence,limitations}}}`. The `target_sha256` is the current Git target digest from `verifier_runner.target_state` through `flow._fingerprint`. Read current material and form judgments with actual evidence references before running. Changes to source before or after execution invalidate affected results. Record model review separately from independent audit and program output.

Synchronizing verification results archives the local result. The Server does not execute project code or receive Verifier definitions through this operation. Add `--submit` only with existing explicit user authorization to synchronize. Without that authorization, local acceptance can proceed. Reads and synchronization reuse the ordinary FormSy login. No Verifier-specific authorization request is required. Enable the submit tool with explicit `--allow-verifier-submit`. The Server checks project write permission for the current account. If permission is insufficient, retain the local receipt and pending synchronization state under the same account and role. `flow verifier-upload PROJECT --operation-id KEY --expected-revision N` retries upload of the same byte-identical receipt for the same `run_id` without rerunning the check. Changed content requires a new `run_id`. Keep Server normalization and historical state after upload. A newly published definition does not replace a pinned local adoption. Investigate and reverify conflicting verdicts for the same definition and target. A transfer failure remains pending synchronization. Server authority `client_reported` records the client report and proves neither independent execution nor business-task completion.

Card actions `knowledge_refresh/knowledge_read/verifier_read/verifier_select/verifier_run/verifier_upload` are pending intents for the current session. Verify project, `run`, `revision`, `input_digests`, and `stage` before invoking the actual command and update the view using its receipt. A click, send, model statement, or historical result does not establish successful execution. Keep the original requirement's `current-run` assignment. For a separate new request, use `enter --switch-from` to switch explicitly. Use `flow select --run-id` to restore the original identity. The switch leaves stage confirmation untouched.

## Server document self-check rules

To check shared project knowledge, run `formsy flow review-rules PROJECT` to read the complete `knowledge_read_constraints kind=project` catalog through the project's own authorized bridge. It belongs to the Knowledge and Verifier supplement. The catalog includes only enabled, project-level mandatory items outside pending scope. Keep built-in SOP methods separate. Read the complete catalog directly instead of substituting recall Top K, a Birdview proposal, or frozen local rules. A successful empty catalog means that no items of this type exist for the project. Record missing configuration, missing authorization, and read failure as distinct gaps. The built-in workflow remains available.

Read against the pinned project binding and preserve continuous pagination plus `content_hash/run_id`. If an item exceeds the page budget, continue from its first page using the catalog's `source_read` bound to the same source. Assemble the original text and verify identity and digest before use. If the catalog changes while reading, discard the partial collection and reread. Preserve the gap on failure.

Map each enabled item's complete original text to an obligation pending judgment. Preserve its original ID, conditions and exceptions, effective amendments, adoption state, and original catalog. The current session decides applicability and result per item from the full source and actual document. Reading alone leaves satisfaction and proposal adoption undecided. Optional `--reference FILE` supports the original `formsy-review-catalog-1` explicitly published by an existing deployment. Use only actual catalog content.

Use the `general` profile to self-check with the original SOP method and actual evidence. For optional project supplements, use `workbench catalog/prepare/scaffold/record --profile server` to save applicability, comparison for each item, and validated citations. Report missing or unchecked material with its actual status. The optional supplement does not gate general self-check. Retain the item, authorized project, run, and document version separately. Recheck affected conclusions when adopted content or the document under review changes. Keep the adopted source pinned when Server publishes a new version.

Acceptance follows the agreed actual local checks and applicable Verifiers. Unrecalled Verifiers can be skipped. An applicable adopted check blocks completion if unrun, unsupported, failed, or stale. Synchronization requires separate authorization. Transfer failure does not invalidate a valid local pass, and conflicting actual verdicts still require resolution. See [the three project supplements](optional-project-context.md).
